<section class="page-hero page-hero-simple seasons-hero"><div><span class="eyebrow">HARVEST & PLACE</span><h1>Seasons are<br><em>regional stories.</em></h1><p>The same fruit can arrive in different months across hemispheres, elevations and rainfall zones. Use this guide to understand the pattern, then confirm the current local harvest.</p></div><div class="simple-hero-side"><span class="big-serif-mark">✦</span><span class="side-rule"></span><p>Months in a year<br>Many harvest windows</p></div></section>
<section class="section-block season-tool-section"><div class="season-tool-intro"><div><span class="eyebrow">SEASON EXPLORER</span><h2>Choose a growing context</h2><p>This is a broad planning guide, not a live market availability report.</p></div><div class="season-controls"><label for="seasonRegion">Growing context</label><select id="seasonRegion"><option value="tropical">Tropical & monsoon climates</option><option value="north">Temperate Northern Hemisphere</option><option value="south">Temperate Southern Hemisphere</option></select></div></div>
<div class="season-panel is-active" data-season-panel="tropical"><div class="season-panel-head"><span>TROPICAL REGIONS</span><h3>Rainfall and flowering often set the rhythm.</h3><p>Mango, lychee, jackfruit and many other tropical crops can fruit in seasonal flushes. Local varieties, monsoon timing and irrigation can make the harvest windows differ even within one country.</p></div><div class="season-fruit-tags"><a href="/fruit/mango">Mango</a><a href="/fruit/lychee">Lychee</a><a href="/fruit/jackfruit">Jackfruit</a><a href="/fruit/guava">Guava</a><a href="/fruit/papaya">Papaya</a><a href="/fruit/pineapple">Pineapple</a></div></div>
<div class="season-panel" data-season-panel="north"><div class="season-panel-head"><span>NORTHERN TEMPERATE</span><h3>Winter dormancy, spring bloom and summer ripening.</h3><p>Apples, pears, plums, cherries and many berries often have harvests that cluster in warm months through autumn. Winter chill and spring frost strongly affect orchard timing.</p></div><div class="season-fruit-tags"><a href="/fruit/apple">Apple</a><a href="/fruit/european-pear">European pear</a><a href="/fruit/blueberry">Blueberry</a><a href="/fruit/strawberry">Strawberry</a><a href="/fruit/plum">Plum</a><a href="/fruit/grape">Grape</a></div></div>
<div class="season-panel" data-season-panel="south"><div class="season-panel-head"><span>SOUTHERN TEMPERATE</span><h3>The calendar shifts with the hemisphere.</h3><p>Many temperate harvests occur in spring and summer in the south, rather than matching familiar Northern Hemisphere calendars. Local cultivars and coastal or inland climates change the dates.</p></div><div class="season-fruit-tags"><a href="/fruit/apple">Apple</a><a href="/fruit/kiwifruit">Kiwifruit</a><a href="/family/stone-fruits">Stone fruit profiles</a><a href="/fruit/grape">Grape</a><a href="/fruit/feijoa">Feijoa</a></div></div>
<div class="season-footnote"><strong>Important:</strong> A fruit being listed here does not guarantee it is in season now. Weather, cultivar, storage and local supply chains all affect availability.</div></section>
<section class="section-block season-method"><div class="section-heading-row"><div><span class="eyebrow">HOW TO READ A HARVEST CALENDAR</span><h2>Four things that change the date</h2></div></div><div class="four-rule-grid"><article><h3>Latitude</h3><p>Day length, average temperature and frost risk vary with latitude and the hemisphere.</p></article><article><h3>Elevation</h3><p>Cooler highlands can shift flowering and maturity compared with warm lowlands.</p></article><article><h3>Rainfall</h3><p>Monsoon and dry seasons can trigger flowering and influence fruit quality in tropical crops.</p></article><article><h3>Cultivar</h3><p>Early, mid- and late-season selections extend harvest windows within a crop.</p></article></div></section>
